title: Shared guide for Definitions 20-21
---
body:

#### About Definition 20

This definition classifies triangles by their symmetries, while definition 21 classifies them by the kinds of angles they contain.

<figure class="diagram">
<canvas id="canvas_0" width="440" height="200" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="I.Def.20 diagram" src="defI20.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    geomlib.init({
        canvasid: "canvas_0",
        background: "0,0,100",
        title: "I.Def.20",
        elements: [
            "A1;point;free;40,160;0",
            "A2;point;free;150,160;0",
            "A;polygon;regularPolygon;A1,A2,3;black;0;black;random",
            "A3;point;vertex;A,3;0",
            "A4;line;angleBisector;A2,A1,A3;0;0;lightGray",
            "A5;line;angleBisector;A2,A3,A1;0;0;lightGray",
            "A6;line;angleBisector;A1,A2,A3;0;0;lightGray",
            "B1;point;free;200,160;0",
            "B2;point;free;270,160;0",
            "B3;point;midpoint;B1,B2;0;0",
            "B4;point;perpendicular;B3,B1;0;0",
            "B5;point;lineSlider;B3,B4,0,40;0",
            "B;polygon;triangle;B1,B2,B5;black;0;black;random",
            "Bline;line;angleBisector;B1,B5,B2;0;0;lightGray",
            "C1;point;free;290,40;0",
            "C2;point;free;370,40;0",
            "C3;point;free;400,160;0",
            "C;polygon;triangle;C1,C2,C3;black;0;black;random"
        ],
    });
</script>
</figure>

The scalene triangle *C* has no symmetries, but the isosceles triangle *B* has a bilateral symmetry. The equilateral triangle *A* not only has three bilateral symmetries, but also has 120°-rotational symmetries.

According to this definition, an equilateral triangle is not to be considered as an isosceles triangle. The term isosceles triangle is first used in proposition @I.5 and later in Books II and IV. The way that the term isosceles triangle is used in the *Elements* does not exclude equilateral triangles. That agrees with modern practice. It is only required that at least two sides be equal in order for a triangle to be isosceles.

Equilateral triangles are constructed in the very first proposition of the Elements, @I.1. An alternate characterization of isosceles triangles, namely that their base angles are equal, is demonstrated in propositions @I.5 and @I.6.

#### About Definition 21

<figure class="diagram">
<canvas id="canvas_1" width="440" height="200" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="I.Def.21 diagram" src="defI21.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    geomlib.init({
        canvasid: "canvas_1",
        background: "0,0,100",
        title: "I.Def.21",
        elements: [
            "D1;point;free;40,40;0",
            "D2;point;free;40,160;0",
            "D3;point;perpendicular;D1,D2;0;0",
            "D4;point;lineSlider;D1,D3,100,30;0",
            "D;polygon;triangle;D1,D2,D4;black;0;black;random",
            "E1;point;free;170,40;0",
            "E2;point;free;170,130;0",
            "G;point;extend;E1,E2,E1,E2;0;0",
            "Gcirc;circle;radius;G,E2;0;0;0;0",
            "E3;point;circleSlider;Gcirc,220,160;0",
            "E;polygon;triangle;E1,E2,E3;black;0;black;random",
            "F1;point;free;300,50;0",
            "F2;point;free;360,160;0",
            "M;point;midpoint;F1,F2;0;0",
            "N;point;perpendicular;M,F2;0;0",
            "O;point;cutoff;M,N,F1,F2;0;0",
            "Ocirc;circle;radius;O,N;0;0;0;0",
            "F3;point;circleSlider;Ocirc,360,30;0",
            "F;polygon;triangle;F1,F2,F3;black;0;black;random"
        ],
    });
</script>
</figure>

Since triangle *D* has a right angle, it is a right triangle. Proposition @I.17 states that the sum of any two angles in a triangle is less than two right angles, therefore, no triangle can contain more than one right angle. Furthermore, there can be at most one obtuse angle, and a right angle and an obtuse angle cannot occur in the same triangle.

Triangle *E* is an obtuse triangle since it has an obtuse angle, while triangle *F* is an acute triangle since all its angles are acute.
